{"packet_id":"a37f76c6031ae701","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from candidate solutions. It lists real alternatives: no expansion, an undifferentiated extension, a channel change and a redesign. It also names B2B relationships and reach to individual buyers as constraints. It does not reach rung 3. It misses that Hellberg already has a consumer-facing product (hlb-junior), and it lists construction and medical as 'consumer' use cases without examining that premise. The €20,000 budget and 12-week timeline do not appear in the case text."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (A9) names who benefits, why behaviour changes and how value is captured: premium pricing, DTC or specialty channels, repeat purchase. The behavioural step is weak. It asserts that consumers will treat hearing protection as a 'fashion item and a status symbol' and buy multiple pairs, with no evidence. The chain exists but rests on speculation, so it does not earn 3."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":3,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (lifestyle/design, outdoor enthusiasts, specialty or DTC channels) and T2 (health/medical necessity, hearing-sensitive adults, medical supply channels) imply different products, customers and channels. However, neither theory addresses the 'no expansion' alternative from A1."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable: WTP premiums of at least 20% or 15%, purchase intent and repeat purchase rate. The thresholds are proposed assumptions, not observations. It is unclear whether failure would seriously threaten each theory. Both theories predict higher repeat purchase and higher purchase intent versus a generic earplug, so those predictions do not distinguish them."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A12","A14","A15","A11"],"reason":"Sources are labelled by class, and 'does not show' limits are stated (A11, A15). But A12 says hlb-junior 'indicates a medical necessity for children', which the excerpt does not say; it lists school, hobby, concerts, lawn mowing and sports. A14 labels bogoch as supporting T2 while its own limit concedes it does not show medical necessity. A14 also recasts 'lack of concern about the appearance of ear plugs' as being 'perceived as non-apparent'. Bogoch's appearance finding bears more on T1's design premise and is never applied there. No assessment considers date, place or fit with the customer group."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13","A14","A15"],"reason":"A13 searches for evidence against T1 and retrieves bogoch, long-2024 and crutzen. Every resulting assessment is directed at T2 (A14, A15), and crutzen is never assessed. Nothing found is reported as bearing on T1. Long-2024's 'never considering HP' and music-quality apathy are relevant challenges to a lifestyle-demand premise but go unweighed against T1."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A17"],"reason":"A17 is costed within the stated constraints. It is not discriminating. The T1 prediction concerns enthusiasts and the T2 prediction concerns hearing-sensitive adults, so both can be true at once and the test cannot separate the theories. The primary measure is WTP for a standard product and a combined 'Lifestyle & Medical' product, which does not isolate either message. The decision rule is truncated ('by at least €10 (or a −'). The test relies only on stated preference."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A19","A9","A20"],"reason":"A19 notes SYN-B and correctly treats it as simulated. It then claims SYN-B 'does not address' T1. T1's stated beneficiaries and value proposition rest on comfort, and A20 sells 'comfort'. A simulated null effect of a softer, better-fitting protector on wear bears at least on that component. Keeping T1 without any qualification is not a proportionate, evidence-linked response."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A18","A20"],"reason":"A20 states reversal conditions and a next action (run X1). But it rates T1 'likely' when no source supports T1 (A11 is neutral), and it recommends investing and a limited launch before the test. The recommendation is internally inconsistent. It favours lifestyle over medical yet targets hearing-sensitive adults, the T2 segment. It also proposes reaching consumers through existing B2B distribution channels without justification."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A12"],"reason":"The hlb-junior excerpt describes a children's product for school, hobby, concert, mowing and sports use. It says nothing about medical necessity, yet A12 asserts that it indicates one."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A14"],"reason":"Bogoch is labelled 'supports' T2 even though the stated limit concedes it does not show medical necessity. The appearance finding is also restated as 'perceived as non-apparent', which the source does not say."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A18","A20"],"reason":"T1 is rated 'likely' and an investment plus limited launch is recommended, but no assessed source supports T1. The only T1 assessment (A11) is neutral, a company aspiration about head protection."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A17"],"reason":"The T1 and T2 predictions concern different subgroups and can both hold, so the test cannot discriminate between them. The primary WTP measure uses a combined lifestyle-and-medical product, and the decision rule is truncated."},{"kind":"unsound_update","event_ids":["A19"],"reason":"SYN-B (simulated) bears on T1's comfort-based behavioural premise. A19 dismisses it as irrelevant and retains T1 entirely, rather than qualifying the comfort component."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A13","A15"],"reason":"The challenge search against T1 yielded only assessments against T2, and crutzen was retrieved but never assessed. The challenge was not reported fairly with respect to the favoured theory."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"The €20,000 budget and 12-week timeline are stated as constraints, but the case text supplies neither."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode completes, but it has several critical errors. Sources are misread as showing medical necessity (A12, A14). It favours T1 as 'likely' and recommends a launch with no supporting evidence (A18, A20). Its test cannot separate the theories (A17), and it dismisses relevant simulated evidence without qualification (A19). Any one of these rejects the trajectory."}
